# CoW WRITE-WALKER corpus · AXIS = how the mutated root is SPELLED at an
# ASSIGNMENT TARGET. CELL SAMPLED HERE: the target is reached through a METHOD
# CHAIN (`outer.get(0).unwrap()[0] = …`), so the root `outer` is not a bare name
# anywhere in the LHS.
#
# The self-host's CoW prescan records which names a function mutates; the
# Branch-C-pre borrow-flip then lets a resource bind alias a collection element
# only while the collection is pristine AFTER the bind. The prescan's
# assignment-target walker had no `EMethodCall` arm, so this spelling marked
# NOTHING: `outer` stayed pristine, `s` was kept as a live Ptr alias into the
# element slot, and the assignment below overwrote the string `s` still points
# at. The program printed the NEW value.
#
# It is a silent WRONG ANSWER, not a crash -- rc 0, and ASan on the emitted C is
# clean, because no memory rule is broken: the alias is valid storage holding
# the wrong string. Core #13's point exactly -- ASan adjudicates memory
# validity, the differential against Rust gg adjudicates value semantics, and
# only the second instrument can see this one.
#
# The fix is the shared `cow_mut_root_name` walk in lower_cow.gg: one
# mutation-path peel that every write-side recorder routes through, so a
# spelling learned at one site cannot be missing at another.
#
# CONTROL: cow_assign_target_named_control.gg -- the same mutation spelled
# through the name `first`, which the walker always marked. It pins that the fix
# is the missing peel and not a blanket over-marking of every assignment.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[Vector[String]] outer = []
    Vector[String] inner = []
    inner.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    outer.push(inner)
    Vector[String] first = outer.get(0).unwrap()
    String s = first.get(0).unwrap()
    outer.get(0).unwrap()[0] = mk("zappedzappedzapped", "zappedzappedzapped")
    print(s)
